  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-\\sqrt{z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{z} & =x+y \\\\ z & =(x+y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,z\u27e9=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,(x+y)^{2}\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=\u27e8u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{2}\u27e9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $(u+v)^{2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that $u+v\\ge 0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
